00:01:07.596 --> 00:01:14.420
This channel would not be what it is if it weren’t&nbsp;
for one very key invention: the microscope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.240
Everything we see, we see with the aid&nbsp;
of light and lenses, expertly deployed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.320
by our master of microscopes, James.
And if you’ve been on this journey from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.120
the beginning, or if you’ve ever gone back to&nbsp;
revisit our earlier videos, you may have noticed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:34.800
that things have changed a bit around here.
Everything we can see is a function of the tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.800
we use. And as we’ve explored new microscopes&nbsp;
along with other new tools and techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:47.440
that means we’ve been able to see the microcosmos&nbsp;
differently. Colors have changed, contrast has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:54.289
deepened, and sometimes things even sparkle.
So today, we’re tracking our journey… through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:01.360
our journey. We have compiled the videos we’ve&nbsp;
made that focus on how our microscopes work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:08.000
and the ways we have improved our methods over time&nbsp;
so you can see how much of microscopy is an art—an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.938
art that uses light in incredible&nbsp;
ways that only physics could predict.&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.960
So to start, let’s go back to an&nbsp;
early video, where we went through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:22.160
the different methods we used at the start of&nbsp;
our channel to illuminate our samples. Think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:28.060
of it like an introduction to optics, told&nbsp;
through the bodies of tardigrades and algae.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:45.120
This is a ciliate, just a eukaryotic microbe&nbsp;
waving its cilia around under our microscope.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.240
This is the same ciliate.
And yup, here it is again…

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.280
…and again.
 But as you’re probably noticing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:58.640
while the rough outline of this organism
seems the same from shot to shot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:05.120
the ciliate itself and the world around it clearly&nbsp;
look very different from shot to shot. Colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:13.020
change, details are more apparent. In one&nbsp;
case the organism seems lit from within.

00:03:13.789 --> 00:03:21.229
On this channel, we're constantly flipping between&nbsp;
different ways of capturing images of organisms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.187
So which one of them is what they&nbsp;
actually look like? Well...none of them.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.360
Anything you see through a microscope is an image&nbsp;
—which in our case, means that everything we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:41.600
show you on this channel, every frame,
is not the microbial world itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.280
It’s an interpretation of life on the other&nbsp;
side of our objective, translated through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:56.457
lens into details, shapes, and colors—all affected&nbsp;
by the way we light up the life we want to see.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:04.720
Light is amazing, it’s also very weird.&nbsp;
It travels in waves, and as it interacts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:12.880
particles and materials, it scatters, shifts. Even&nbsp;
if we can’t actually see those light waves in motion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:19.840
so much of what we observe in the world&nbsp;
around us is rooted in the physical properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:27.600
that define those waves—like how we can observe&nbsp;
certain frequencies of light as colors. But waves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.320
have far more to them than&nbsp;
just their frequency, and microscopy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:39.200
has combined the resourcefulness of many&nbsp;
different sciences to use light to give us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:48.400
different ways to peer into the microbial world. 
So let’s start simple with the good old fashioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:56.160
white light. Early microscopists used oil lamps&nbsp;
and sunlight to see through their microscopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.560
and while the technology has changed, the&nbsp;
simplicity of this has endured into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:06.320
the modern technique of brightfield microscopy.
It all starts with a source of light, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:12.480
modern microscopes have their lamps built in, set&nbsp;
up underneath the stage that holds our sample.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.920
Light travels from the source through&nbsp;
a condenser, which works to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.480
the light onto the sample above it.
This focused light travels through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:29.600
sample towards the objective lens, which takes&nbsp;
in an image and magnifies it into these bright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:35.920
backgrounds with organisms, sometimes rendered&nbsp;
transparent by the intensity of the light.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:42.400
You might say that this is as close as we get&nbsp;
to seeing what the microcosmos actually looks like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.546
but that would be like taking 2000 watt&nbsp;
light bulb into your living room and saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:54.240
“this is what my home looks like.” Light affects&nbsp;
things, and we are not shining light on these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:06:00.160
organisms, we’re shining light through them.
Now, brightfield might seem relatively simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:08.080
but that simplicity has been incredibly powerful&nbsp;
in allowing scientists old and new to wade through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:14.560
microscopic waters. Still, there are limitations&nbsp;
to consider with any scientific technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.800
and one of the major challenges for&nbsp;
brightfield microscopy, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:26.560
when we’re looking at microbes, is contrast.
Pigmented organisms are easy to visualize against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.680
the bright background, but in cases where&nbsp;
the organism has been rendered transparent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.331
it can be harder to distinguish their bodies&nbsp;
from the rest of the world they inhabit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.320
Scientists can navigate these challenges using&nbsp;
stains that make certain structures more visible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.384
but for our purposes, we like to avoid stains&nbsp;
because they can affect the microbes themselves.

00:06:48.924 --> 00:06:52.124
There are ways to contend with this challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:58.560
one of which is built on one of those&nbsp;
simple-yet-strange properties of our world:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:05.920
you don’t always need to shine light directly&nbsp;
onto an object to see it. This technique is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:12.400
darkfield microscopy, which sounds like it&nbsp;
must be the opposite of brightfield microscopy.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.313
It’s not. The two techniques&nbsp;
are actually very similar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:20.080
light travels from a source through a condenser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:26.034
interacts with the sample, and then travels into&nbsp;
the objective lens, producing the image we see.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:34.195
But what we want in darkfield microscopy is for the&nbsp;
beam of light to hit the sample, but not our eye.
 &nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.880
In darkfield microscopy, a circular&nbsp;
disk is placed inside the condenser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:47.760
blocking the central part of the light light from&nbsp;
shining through the sample and into our eye, or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:53.840
our camera. This means that when there is no&nbsp;
sample on the slide, all you’ll see is black.
 &nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:02.720
But the disk doesn’t block all light: there&nbsp;
is still a hollow cone of light that travels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:10.640
around the disk, unable to reach the objective or&nbsp;
our eyes, but that light still hits the sample.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:18.000
When it does, their microscopic, transparent&nbsp;
bodies scatter those hidden rays into&nbsp;our

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:24.480
view. And as they do, an image of their&nbsp;
bodies forms against a dark background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:32.240
providing us with this almost cinematic footage.
Another method to get better contrast than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.800
brightfield microscopy is called&nbsp;
phase contrast microscopy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:43.200
and it’s built on working with a property of&nbsp;
light that we can’t actually directly experience.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:49.440
Microbes , or really anything, that is easily&nbsp;
visually observed with brightfield microscopy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.920
are called amplitude objects because&nbsp;
as light passes through them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:59.600
the amplitude of the light wave changes,&nbsp;
which we see as changes in light intensity.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:06.080
But there is another class of specimens: these&nbsp;
are called phase objects. As light passes through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:13.600
these objects, the waves slow down and shift&nbsp;
slightly in phase compared to the unaffected light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.480
around it. And if you’re wondering what that means&nbsp;
in terms of what we can see, that’s the issue:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:25.520
our eyes don’t process these differences&nbsp;
in phase. And so in the final image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:29.600
these objects, or in our case,&nbsp;
organisms, are very difficult to see.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:41.120
In the 1930s, a physicist named Frits Zernike&nbsp;
developed a method to shift the direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:48.000
light just slightly enough so that these&nbsp;
changes in phase could actually be translated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:54.320
into changes in amplitude, producing&nbsp;
an image of these formerly hard-to-see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.320 --> 00:10:00.160
phase objects by essentially treating them&nbsp;
as amplitude objects. There is a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:04.480
physics to this that we are glossing&nbsp;
over, but the result was so important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.680
that it would eventually win Zernike the&nbsp;
Nobel Prize in Physics. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:17.840
selfishly, we appreciate his work because it lets&nbsp;
us see more of our more hidden microbial friends.
 &nbsp;

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:25.680
And for the last type of microscopy we’ll go over&nbsp;
today, we’re going to be getting into another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:32.320
property of light that we can’t directly&nbsp;
see, but that makes the microcosmos glow.
 &nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.640
Most of the light we see has an electrical field&nbsp;
that vibrates in all sorts of planes relative to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:45.520
the direction the light is traveling in. But&nbsp;
that vibration can be restricted to one plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:52.000
and when that happens, the light is said to&nbsp;
be polarized. We[8] can’t see the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.800
between polarized and unpolarized light.&nbsp;
You might notice the difference in how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.440
world looks when you’re wearing polarized&nbsp;
sunglasses, but these are changes brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:07.120
about by changes in color or intensity,&nbsp;
not the polarization of the light itself.
 &nbsp;

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:13.600
So when does polarized light help us&nbsp;
in microscopy? Well, a lot of materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:19.280
stay the same optically-speaking, no matter what&nbsp;
direction you shoot light at them with. But there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:26.480
are certain materials where specific properties,&nbsp;
like how fast light travels through them, can vary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:33.440
depending on which way the light is striking them.&nbsp;
These materials. called optically anisotropic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:40.000
can also take in a ray of light and&nbsp;
divide it into two separate beams.
 &nbsp;

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:46.960
By aiming polarized light at our sample&nbsp;
and then reconstructing an image based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:52.080
how the various parts of the organism&nbsp;
interacts with that restricted light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:58.400
particularly by how it might cause the light&nbsp;
to split, we can see more of these optically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:09.840
anisotropic materials in action. In our case,&nbsp;
it often takes the form of shiny crystals.
 &nbsp;

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:17.520
So we’ve given you the big overview of&nbsp;
what these different techniques can do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:22.880
let’s go back and review what that means&nbsp;
for the original ciliate we started with.
 &nbsp;

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:28.480
This is the ciliate under brightfield microscopy.&nbsp;
The background is bright, the image produced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:34.240
changes in light amplitude that allow us to see&nbsp;
the overall shape. But some of the detail is hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:41.280
to make out. Under darkfield though, the contrast&nbsp;
increases and some of these details become more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:48.240
obvious, displaying compartments and cilia in&nbsp;
greater detail that are also apparent under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:55.040
phase contrast. And then under polarized light,&nbsp;
the crystals that blended in with the organism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.040 --> 00:13:03.360
previously are now quite visible and vibrant.
 
There are, of course, many other microscopy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:13.120
techniques, but we think it’s incredible that&nbsp;
with just these four, the world of the microcosmos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:20.000
looks almost like different universes,&nbsp;
wrapped up in one invisible world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:28.000
around us. The journey, it seems, is not&nbsp;
just about what you see, but how you see it.

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:35.440
And ultimately, none of these views are&nbsp;
what the microcosmos actually looks like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:43.280
either that, or all of them are. Our brains play&nbsp;
tricks on us to make us believe that the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:49.280
looks one way, but the world looks different&nbsp;
at night than in the day, and both of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:56.720
things have more to do with the physiology&nbsp;
of our eyes than their objective reality.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:03.520
Asking what microbes look like is, to&nbsp;
some extent, forcing our own experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:08.960
onto something that is beyond it. Which is&nbsp;
not something I ever would have thought of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:12.000
if it weren’t for this little YouTube channel.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:18.960
Now we have been really fortunate over the years&nbsp;
to have the support of so many people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:23.520
and to be able to direct that support&nbsp;
back to the microscopes we use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:29.520
In 2020, we introduced James’ new microscope,&nbsp;
which he was able to buy thanks to crowdfunding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:36.000
This was our first major microscope upgrade on&nbsp;
the series, and it’s incredible to see the jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:42.000
in quality and how it compares to the different&nbsp;
techniques we showcased in the previous video.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:15:00.080
This golden algae is swimming around&nbsp;
in water and white light, illuminated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:06.640
from below using a brightfield microscope.
And here we have that mallomonas sample,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:11.840
filmed a day later using a different&nbsp;
technique thanks to a brand new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:18.240
freshly installed microscope.
When James, our master of microscopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:23.440
saw them for the first time, he got a little&nbsp;
emotional because, to quote him directly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:31.120
“the thing I know so well, was looking&nbsp;
so so much different than usual.”&nbsp;

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:41.360
Those differences in how the mallomonas look are&nbsp;
due to a type of microscopy called “Differential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:48.480
Interference Contrast.” Or…DIC.
Earlier this year, James started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:53.520
a crowdfunding campaign to purchase a&nbsp;
new microscope, one that would allow him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:59.280
to use differential interference contrast&nbsp;
to produce these images you are seeing now.&nbsp;

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:07.040
Now technically, “differential interference&nbsp;
contrast” is not a type of microscope. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:15.440
it’s a method that enhances contrast. And as you&nbsp;
can see, the final product is an image that seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:24.160
almost 3-dimensional. Just watch this stentor&nbsp;
coeruleus as it swims across the slide. Its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:28.080
cilia are so prominent that&nbsp;
it almost feels like you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:34.240
touch their vibrating fuzziness. And&nbsp;
the striations down its body are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:41.040
sharp that when the stentor contracts, it’s&nbsp;
like you can feel it pushing inward and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:49.783
back out again. I don’t know about you,&nbsp;
but it’s almost like you could reach out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.783 --> 00:16:49.877
and touch it—like you’re in a movie theater&nbsp;
with 3d glasses on, reaching out to something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.877 --> 00:16:53.280
that seems to be reaching back out to you.
Differential interference contrast microscopy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.280 --> 00:17:00.480
was invented by Georges Nomarski in 1952,&nbsp;
building off the principles underlying phase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:07.440
contrast microscopy, which is a different though&nbsp;
similar thing. But both of these methods work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:13.920
to translate invisible shifts in light phase that&nbsp;
can happen when studying certain samples into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:21.040
visible changes in light amplitude, which we see&nbsp;
as changes in light intensity. And as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:28.160
both techniques enhance contrast and let us see&nbsp;
parts of the microcosmos that might be invisible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:35.280
with regular brightfield microscopy. But phase&nbsp;
contrast microscopy and DIC shift and work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:42.720
light in different ways to accomplish this goal.
How do they do it? Well, if you’ve ever taken an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:49.680
optics course, you will remember how terrible&nbsp;
that was. But, very basically, phase contrast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:57.440
and DIC are two ways to take advantage of&nbsp;
a strange reality. Light actually travels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:05.440
further through some materials than others. Kinda.&nbsp;
The optical path length is a function of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:12.800
the distance between two points and the refractive&nbsp;
index of the material the light is travelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:21.520
through. Basically, denser samples have longer&nbsp;
optical path lengths. Phase contrast microscopy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:26.960
takes advantage of that, making areas with&nbsp;
longer optical path lengths look darker.&nbsp;

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:34.400
DIC on the other hand, doesn’t make areas&nbsp;
that are denser darker, it uses some very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:42.960
cool optics to create sharp contrast in areas&nbsp;
of rapid change in the optical path length.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:51.360
So, the faster the gradient from more to less&nbsp;
dense or vice versa, the more contrast you see.&nbsp;

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:57.120
But it’s also important to note that&nbsp;
while these images are the result of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:03.680
traveling through a sample, they are not&nbsp;
an actual topographical map of an organism.&nbsp;

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:11.440
DIC microscopy is a technique, but it does require&nbsp;
certain physical additions to a microscope. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:18.320
one thing, we need to split light apart. So,&nbsp;
first we have a prism that splits polarized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:25.280
light into two orthogonal rays of light that will&nbsp;
then pass through the sample and interact with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:31.280
in different ways. And then there’s a second&nbsp;
prism that recombines those two rays after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:37.200
they’ve traveled through the sample, forming an&nbsp;
image based on the differences those two beams of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:45.200
light experienced for our eyes and cameras to see.
In addition to these prisms, this technique relies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:50.560
on having higher quality objectives than what&nbsp;
we’ve been using in the past. The objectives are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:58.000
the little lenses at the bottom of the microscope&nbsp;
that actually do most of the magnification. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:03.840
many people would realize this, but the objectives&nbsp;
we use for Journey to the Microcosmos are some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:11.040
the cheapest and most common types of objectives,&nbsp;
they’re known as achromatic objectives. But while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:18.560
these objectives produce so many of our favorite&nbsp;
images, James needed a microscope update to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:25.600
able to do differential interference contrast.
So with help, perhaps including some from you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:33.600
James bought a new Zeiss microscope. It took eight&nbsp;
weeks for it to arrive, which is a long time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:42.960
wait. But luckily the manufacturers sent him a 150&nbsp;
page manual to occupy his time until it arrived.&nbsp;

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:50.720
When the microscope finally showed up, James still&nbsp;
had to wait for an engineer to install the nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:58.880
50 pound machine. But he didn’t waste any time.&nbsp;
James went out to gather his precious Mallomonas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:05.520
and prepared a slide that night, keeping it stored&nbsp;
in a humidity chamber so that he could look at it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:10.480
as soon as the microscope was ready.
The next day, the Zeiss engineer took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:15.840
3.5 hours to put all the pieces together&nbsp;
and set everything up. But from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:29.840
well, it was all on James to observe what he&nbsp;
could and experiment with his new microscope.&nbsp;

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:46.000
Some of his experimenting has been geared towards&nbsp;
making sure that he can get the best image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:52.880
possible. These objectives have a much smaller&nbsp;
working distance compared to our previous ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:57.840
and so you need to prepare the slides&nbsp;
a lot thinner to get a sharper image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:02.880
So even for our master of microscopes,&nbsp;
there was somewhat of a learning curve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:07.760
to make sure he was getting videos that&nbsp;
were as sharp and vivid as he wanted.&nbsp;

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:12.080
But some of the experimenting is&nbsp;
based out of that most exciting thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:18.240
of all: curiosity. He’s removed some of the&nbsp;
prisms to see how it affects the footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:25.840
and added a magnifying glass over the light&nbsp;
source to scatter the light in different ways.&nbsp;

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:36.160
That’s the joy of the microcosmos. It is so&nbsp;
infinite. There’s it’s own objective existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:39.840
full of so many more organisms than&nbsp;
we’ll ever be able to identify.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:45.920
But there’s also the infinite&nbsp;
nature of how we experience it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:53.840
of how our own view of this world is shaped by&nbsp;
the many different tools we use to observe it.&nbsp;

00:22:54.400 --> 00:22:58.320
And maybe that’s the case all&nbsp;
the time...that what we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:05.200
is as much about how we view the world as it is&nbsp;
about the world itself. The same thing with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:10.640
different lens, a different technique, a different&nbsp;
base of knowledge, can look completely different.

00:23:11.822 --> 00:23:16.668
As we start our third season of 
Journey to the Microcosmos, we hope

00:23:16.668 --> 00:23:22.073
you will continue to join us through 
both our new and old lenses,

00:23:22.073 --> 00:23:27.147
as we uncover more of this hidden, unending world.

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:36.560
In 2021, James went all out on improving his&nbsp;
toolkit, investing in higher quality objectives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:43.040
a polarizer, and the equipment he needed to do&nbsp;
fluorescence microscopy. If you’re not sure why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:47.840
those things had all of us on the Journey&nbsp;
to the Microcosmos team so excited, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:53.440
that’s what our next video is for. You will get to&nbsp;
see just how much of a difference an objective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.440 --> 00:24:01.712
can make, and why electrons jumping up and down&nbsp;
can reveal a whole new side to the microcosmos.

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:19.360
We’re nearing the end of our fourth season of&nbsp;
Journey to the Microcosmos, and over that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:23.440
we’ve been able to see microbes through&nbsp;
all kinds of lenses—literally—thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:28.960
to James, our master of microscopes. For&nbsp;
the rest of us on the Microcosmos team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:33.840
it’s been really cool to not only see&nbsp;
the footage that James has recorded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:38.560
but also see the journey that he has&nbsp;
been on with the microscopes themselves.&nbsp;

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:43.040
Now, we’ve been able to make this journey&nbsp;
because of all of you and the support you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:48.320
have shown to this channel by watching and&nbsp;
commenting and sharing with others, or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:53.600
and especially by supporting us on Patreon.&nbsp;
We’ve all been on this journey together, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:59.840
let’s look back on where it’s taken us, and also&nbsp;
get a peek at where we’re going in the future.&nbsp;

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:10.720
In the first season, James started out with&nbsp;
two microscopes. One was a microscope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:17.440
he assembled himself from different parts for&nbsp;
under $200. The other microscope was a Motic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:22.720
BA310 that he received from a microscope&nbsp;
company shortly after we started this channel.&nbsp;

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:28.080
And these microscopes give us so much beautiful&nbsp;
footage, thanks in part to the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:34.880
techniques that James used with them, whether that&nbsp;
was bright-field or phase contrast or darkfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:40.400
or some other technique to manipulate&nbsp;
the light shining on the microcosmos.&nbsp;

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:45.680
But these microscopes also had their&nbsp;
limits, so we were really excited to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:51.040
what would happen when James bought a new&nbsp;
microscope before the start of our third season:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:56.640
the Zeiss Axioscope 5. This new microscope&nbsp;
came with higher quality objectives (which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.640 --> 00:26:00.560
are the lenses on the bottom of the&nbsp;
microscope), as well as prisms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:06.640
made it possible for us to look at microbes with&nbsp;
differential interference contrast microscopy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:12.560
a technique that sharpened their features and made&nbsp;
them seem more three-dimensional on our screen.&nbsp;

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:17.680
We have videos exploring these different&nbsp;
techniques and microscopes, so if you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:25.520
learn more about them, we recommend checking those&nbsp;
out because light is much weirder than it seems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:30.080
But we wanted to do a quick summary for reasons&nbsp;
that are probably very clear from the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:38.400
of this video: we have upgraded our microscope!
The Zeiss Axioscope 5 was a very exciting change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:44.400
but it was also an expensive one. So when James&nbsp;
bought it, he was only able to get two objectives:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:51.600
a 20x objective and a 63x objective. Adding that&nbsp;
to the 10x magnification the eyepiece gives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:59.360
let us magnify our samples by 200x and 630x&nbsp;
respectively, which is not bad. But a standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:06.560
microscope usually comes with 5-6 objectives for&nbsp;
a reason: the microcosmos is full of things both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:11.120
small and large to observe and there&nbsp;
isn’t a one-size-fits-all objective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:16.480
that you can use to observe everything.
So one of James’ priorities was expanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:22.160
his set of objectives so that he could have&nbsp;
the flexibility to see the microcosmos at more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:28.560
scales. And thanks to his recent upgrade,&nbsp;
he’s now gone from a set of 2 to a set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:38.240
six that range from 5x to 100x and that gives&nbsp;
us a magnification range from 50x to 1000x.&nbsp;

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:44.320
Since I have gotten my own prototype&nbsp;
of the microcosmos microscope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:49.920
we are manufacturing thanks to backers of our&nbsp;
kickstarter, I’ve been amazed at how important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:56.000
it is to have this variety of options, and how&nbsp;
much time I spend on the lower magnifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:59.920
and well as going deeper .
So without further ado,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:05.200
let’s introduce these different objectives by&nbsp;
looking at a ciliate called Tetrahymena that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:19.840
taken up residence in a rotifer exoskeleton.
With the 5x objective, we can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:25.520
little dots swimming in what used to be empty&nbsp;
space, but it’s hard to make out the details.&nbsp;

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:30.800
When we move to the 10x objective, we can&nbsp;
start to make out shapes of the Tetrahymena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:35.520
and more of the emptiness around them,&nbsp;
but it’s difficult to see the exoskeleton.&nbsp;

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:42.080
Then as we start to go up in magnification,&nbsp;
the details of the exoskeleton become clearer.&nbsp;

00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:48.000
It's cool to see more and more of those details&nbsp;
as we zoom in. And especially when we get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:55.280
the 100x objective, which gives us 1000x&nbsp;
magnification, you can make out so much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.280 --> 00:29:01.520
the insides of the Tetrahymena, that they&nbsp;
look like bags of very resilient bubbles.&nbsp;

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:11.360
Here’s another ciliate called a Pseudoprorodon&nbsp;
that James looked at through all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:16.640
objectives. At first it just looks like a little&nbsp;
green thing floating around and knocking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:22.800
stuff. But as we go up in magnification, we can&nbsp;
start to make out the subtle undulations of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:29.600
body, which shifts the different shades of green.&nbsp;
And as we look even closer, the green itself takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:36.960
on a new life, revealing the perimeters of the&nbsp;
individual algae inside the pseudoprorodon.&nbsp;

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:43.440
Revealing those details inside the tetrahymena&nbsp;
and pseudoprorodon comes at a cost. We can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:47.280
see as much of the world around them,&nbsp;
whether that’s the emptiness around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.280 --> 00:29:52.480
rotifer exoskeleton or the objects that&nbsp;
the pseudoprorodon keeps bumping into.&nbsp;

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:59.200
Whether or not you need those details depends on&nbsp;
what you’re trying to learn or what we are trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.200 --> 00:30:04.960
to show, and that’s why having the flexibility&nbsp;
to look through a wider range of objectives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:13.600
is such an exciting upgrade for us. Objectives are&nbsp;
our gateways to the microcosmos, and we’re excited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:20.160
to have all of these new scales to look at.
We’ve also made some other upgrades so we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:25.360
revisit techniques we used on our older&nbsp;
microscopes. Like a polarizer, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:31.680
who doesn’t love this shiny, iridescent stuff?
But we’re not just sticking with what we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:37.440
known. Thanks to this upgrade, we’ve got a&nbsp;
whole new way to look at cells: fluorescence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:45.040
microscopy. And we are so excited for this one.
The techniques we’ve highlighted so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:51.040
are all clever manipulations of white light, a&nbsp;
mixture of many visible wavelengths of light. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:56.640
in fluorescence microscopy, instead of&nbsp;
illuminating our sample with that mixture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.640 --> 00:31:03.040
we choose a specific wavelength of light.
That light is called the excitation light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:08.080
because, well, it excites certain&nbsp;
structures based on their chemistry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:13.920
And those excitable structures then emit some&nbsp;
light of their own. The emission light will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:19.520
then travel back through the objective, get past&nbsp;
barriers that filter out the excitation light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:25.920
and then show up in our eyes as vivid color.
With our upgrade, we have four different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:30.160
wavelengths of light that we can&nbsp;
use for fluorescence microscopy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:35.680
and the parts of the sample that we see&nbsp;
fluoresce will depend on which light we use.&nbsp;

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:40.800
In this footage, we’re looking at the&nbsp;
Pseudoprorodon that we saw earlier, only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:48.720
this time it’s being hit with 385 nanometer light.&nbsp;
The red we see is the fluorescence from natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:53.920
structures, this is called autofluorescence.&nbsp;
The rest of the organism is dark because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.920 --> 00:32:01.280
it did not respond to that 385 nanometer&nbsp;
light, making the color all the more vibrant.&nbsp;

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:07.360
But the power of fluorescence microscopy is&nbsp;
not just finding fluorescent structures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:12.320
exist in nature. Chemists and biologists&nbsp;
have developed all sorts of ingenious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:17.760
techniques to highlight different parts of&nbsp;
cells using fluorescent dyes and proteins.&nbsp;

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:24.080
James is working on building up his arsenal of&nbsp;
fluorescence microscopy tools and techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.080 --> 00:32:30.800
so that soon, we’ll be able to dive even deeper&nbsp;
into the microcosmos to seek out things that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:41.840
been hidden from us before, uncovering the unseen&nbsp;
world inside the unseen world that surrounds us

00:32:43.040 --> 00:32:46.080
The upgrades we’ve talked about in&nbsp;
the last few videos have involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:49.440
fancy equipment and exciting techniques. But light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:54.880
is so weird and powerful that sometimes you don’t&nbsp;
need anything particularly fancy to make it do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.880 --> 00:33:00.720
something useful. Sometimes you just need&nbsp;
the right objective and a little bit of oil.

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:12.480
If you’ve been following along with us lately,&nbsp;
you probably know that we’re pretty excited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:18.640
about our latest microscope upgrades. And can&nbsp;
you blame us? It's like having a new toy that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:23.680
actually a spaceship. It’s pretty hard to&nbsp;
shut up about something that is shiny and new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:28.720
and also takes you into a whole new world.
And that’s kind of what it feels like to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:34.640
our familiar friends at this magnification. Even&nbsp;
though we’ve seen tardigrades many times before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:38.240
in so many different ways, we&nbsp;
have not seen them like this.&nbsp;

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:49.600
And bringing this level of magnification gives&nbsp;
us a new layer of the microcosmos to explore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.600 --> 00:33:55.360
It’s like we’ve descended into a new level of&nbsp;
a cave, getting further from our world—though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.360 --> 00:33:59.760
fortunately for the easily scared among&nbsp;
us, the cave is metaphorical and the risks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:06.240
are negligible. All we need for the journey are&nbsp;
some objective lenses and a tiny drop of... oil?&nbsp;

00:34:07.280 --> 00:34:10.560
Yeah, oil.
But to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.200 --> 00:34:15.200
what that oil does, we’re going to have to&nbsp;
get a little technical first. Because yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.200 --> 00:34:20.560
we love our microbes, but we’re only able&nbsp;
to see them thanks to light, and light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:26.480
is complicated, but we need to give it its due.
So let’s start with the objective lens.&nbsp;

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:31.520
The main job of the objective lens&nbsp;
is to take light leaving the sample&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:37.120
and focus it into an image for the us&nbsp;
to see. And there are two main numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:37.120 --> 00:34:42.960
that describe the objective lens. The first one’s&nbsp;
pretty obvious: it’s the magnification. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:47.360
magnification you see written on the objective&nbsp;
lens is not the same as the magnification we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:51.600
write up on the corner of the videos.
That’s because the objective lens isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:55.840
the only source of magnification in our&nbsp;
microscope. We’re also looking through an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.840 --> 00:35:01.120
eyepiece that has its own magnification. So the&nbsp;
final magnification that we see is the product of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:06.640
the objective lens’ and eyepiece’s magnifications.
Now we don’t usually do math on this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:10.640
but this is one of the rare cases where&nbsp;
the math and optics is straightforward.&nbsp;

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:15.440
In our microscope, the eyepiece has a&nbsp;
magnification of 10x. So if we’re looking through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:22.480
a 63x objective, the final magnification is 630x.&nbsp;
And that is the number you’ll see in the corner.&nbsp;

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:29.680
But you should never trust an optics lesson that&nbsp;
seems simple. How could it be when the image we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:38.160
seeing is the product of so many things at once,&nbsp;
some tangible like a lens, others much less so.&nbsp;

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:44.000
And what is less tangible than&nbsp;
light, which is, as we’ve seen before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:51.520
on this channel--a very strange thing. We&nbsp;
cannot touch it, but we can manipulate it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:58.320
using what we know of the way it travels and&nbsp;
bends and reflects to see our world differently.&nbsp;

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:05.360
But light… has its limits. The thing about&nbsp;
making things larger is that at some point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:10.320
it’s not enough to just zoom in. You need to&nbsp;
be able to capture the detail that’s there.&nbsp;

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:15.840
And this leads to the second important&nbsp;
number you’ll find on an objective lens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:22.320
the numerical aperture. Unlike magnification,&nbsp;
“numerical aperture” is probably not super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:28.880
self-explanatory. What the number describes is&nbsp;
the ability of the objective to take in light.&nbsp;

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:34.560
As light travels from the sample and to&nbsp;
the objective, it radiates outwards like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:40.640
a cone. The higher your numerical aperture, the&nbsp;
wider the cone of light that's going into your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:40.640 --> 00:36:46.960
objective. And that allows for more rays to enter&nbsp;
the objective from all sorts of different angles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:52.400
helping to illuminate more details and give&nbsp;
greater resolution to your final image.&nbsp;

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:57.040
But of course, that’s still not all that goes&nbsp;
into capturing the perfect microscopic image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:07.600
Let’s take a look at this glaucoma spinning around&nbsp;
at 1000x magnification. It’s visible, and you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:07.600 --> 00:37:11.680
see some of the details. It looks…fine.
But let’s take a look at it again here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:17.760
Same glaucoma. Same objective. Same level&nbsp;
of magnification. What was “fine” before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:23.440
now just looks dull in comparison to the image we&nbsp;
were seeing before with the detailed striations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:28.560
and vivid pockets of green on the glaucoma’s body.
To get from one of these images to the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:34.000
James—our master of microscopes—didn’t change&nbsp;
any of the technical settings on his microscope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:40.320
or pull some kind of video editing wizardry. He&nbsp;
just simply added a drop of oil to the coverslip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:45.360
encasing the glaucoma, and dipped&nbsp;
the objective into the oil.&nbsp;

00:37:46.080 --> 00:37:53.200
Now this is because the cone of light going from&nbsp;
the specimen into the objective isn’t just going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.200 --> 00:37:59.760
straight from the sample to the lens. It’s passing&nbsp;
through something—it’s passing through air.&nbsp;

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:06.480
Light travels at different speeds through&nbsp;
different materials and when it changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:12.880
from material to material, it will bend. This&nbsp;
is called refraction and materials like oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:16.800
slow light down more.
And that matters to our microscope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:22.080
because it gives us the wider cones of light&nbsp;
we need to get a higher numerical aperture.&nbsp;

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:27.600
Whether we’ve got air between the sample and&nbsp;
the lens or oil when we’re viewing our samples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:32.400
depends on the objective we’re looking through.&nbsp;
Our lower magnification objectives have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:37.600
lower numerical apertures, and they’re meant&nbsp;
to be used with air. Our higher magnification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.600 --> 00:38:42.240
objectives have higher numerical apertures,&nbsp;
and they’re meant to be used with oil.&nbsp;

00:38:42.880 --> 00:38:48.800
Using air with oil objectives gives us poor&nbsp;
images, and using oil with our dry objective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:54.400
would damage the objective. So…don’t do that.
The numerical aperture and its relationship to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.400 --> 00:39:00.320
resolution is important because it puts a limit&nbsp;
on just how much magnification we can actually do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.320 --> 00:39:05.360
with a given objective. There’s a general estimate&nbsp;
if you are looking into microscopy yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.360 --> 00:39:10.480
that you’ll get a good magnification when you’re&nbsp;
working in the range that’s 500 to 1000 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.480 --> 00:39:16.480
the numerical aperture of the objective.
So our 100x objective and 1.3 numerical aperture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:22.720
can reach a maximum of 1300x magnification&nbsp;
depending on your eyepiece. With our 10x eyepiece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:28.480
we get 1000x magnification, and it works great.
But if we tried to get more from this set-up by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:33.760
increasing the magnification of our eyepiece&nbsp;
to 20x, we could not actually get 2000x&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:38.640
magnification. Because of the constraints&nbsp;
of our numerical aperture, we would just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:43.840
be losing half of our image without actually&nbsp;
gaining more detail in what’s left to see.&nbsp;

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:48.800
But that’s what happens any time we want to&nbsp;
take an image: we have to make a decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:56.800
between what we can and cannot see. There are&nbsp;
always choices that have to be made, and details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.800 --> 00:40:04.080
that have to be lost. We simply cannot see all&nbsp;
of the world, in all its entirety, all at once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:11.760
But we can see more of it by tracking the choices&nbsp;
we make as we dive deeper, choices in techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:18.320
and materials that affect what we can see and&nbsp;
how we see it, and enrich the story further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:25.120
even when those choices impose constraints.
At some point in history, we wanted to see more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:25.120 --> 00:40:31.600
and lenses with their magnificent manipulation of&nbsp;
light have helped us do that. We’ve used them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:38.560
see light from distant stars, and to peer into the&nbsp;
most mundane surroundings on earth. And whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:44.560
image has come back to us has brought the universe&nbsp;
closer to view, even if it’s just in fractions.

